WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.570
At Kinnitty, in County Offaly, a long
ridge of sand and gravel is exposed: an

2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.810
esker.

3
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.430
Eskers are usually metres
to tens of metres high.

4
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.870
Some run for hundreds of kilometres.

5
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.430
Cut into one and the stones
are rounded, sorted by size,

6
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.230
and laid in layers.

7
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.790
That's the work of water, not ice.

8
00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.570
Yet some eskers wind
uphill, and over hills.

9
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.370
Rivers only flow downhill.

10
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.610
Did the hills rise later?

11
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.790
So how can river gravel climb?

12
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.630
In this mission, you'll learn to tell
ice-laid till from water-laid gravel.

13
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.290
Then you'll read an
outwash plain in Iceland,

14
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.270
where gravel gives way to sand downstream.

15
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:46.840
Your mission: explain how a ridge of river
gravel can wind uphill and over hills.
